---
import PNotes from "../components/PNotes.astro";
---

<p-slide class="cover-internal-sub-section">
  <h2>One more thing: WebMCP</h2>

  <PNotes>
    <p>Epilogue, "one more thing" style. No deep tech — just a heads-up.</p>
    <p>
      We just spent the whole talk making interfaces smarter at runtime. Now a
      question that's a bit uncomfortable for a room of designers and
      frontenders.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3 class="main-gradient">Your UI has a new user</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      🤖 <span>Agents today </span><span class="main-sub-gradient"
        >guess</span
      ><span> your interface — screenshots, clicks</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🧰 <span>With WebMCP the page </span><span class="main-sub-gradient"
        >declares</span
      ><span> what it can do, as tools</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      🎯 <span>No guessing: the agent </span><span class="main-sub-gradient"
        >calls the action</span
      >
    </li>
  </ul>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Chrome's own definition: "a proposed web standard to help you build and
      expose structured tools for AI agents".
    </p>
    <p>
      Today a browser agent scrapes the DOM and simulates clicks and typing —
      it guesses. With WebMCP the site says: here is "book a table", here are
      its fields. The agent calls a tool instead of guessing.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h5 style="margin-block-end: 1em" class="center">
    <span class="main-gradient">Two attributes. A new user.</span> 🧰
  </h5>
  <pre
    class="small-line"
    data-label-file="booking.html">
    <code style="font-size: 0.65em" is:raw class="language-scss">
    &lt;form toolname="bookTable"
          tooldescription="Book a table for dinner"&gt;</code>
  </pre>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Two attributes turn an existing form into a tool: toolname and
      tooldescription. The form fields become the tool's parameters.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the declarative side. There's also a JavaScript side
      (document.modelContext.registerTool) — explain it here in your own words.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide support>
  <h3 class="main-gradient">Where it stands</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      🧪 <span>Proposed standard — </span><span class="main-sub-gradient"
        >W3C Community Group draft</span
      >
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🌐 <span>Origin trial in </span><span class="main-sub-gradient"
        >Chrome 149+</span
      >
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      🌱 <span>Early days — </span><span class="main-sub-gradient"
        >watch it, try it</span
      ><span>, don't bet on it yet</span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Spec: Draft Community Group Report of the W3C Web Machine Learning
      Community Group — explicitly not a W3C Standard, not on the standards
      track. Editors from Microsoft and Google.
    </p>
    <p>
      Chrome docs say it's "under active discussion and subject to change".
      Local testing: chrome://flags/#enable-webmcp-testing.
    </p>
    <p>
      At I/O 2026 Google showed brands experimenting with it: Expedia,
      Booking.com, Shopify, Etsy, Target and others. Experimenting — not
      shipped.
    </p>
    <p>
      Don't claim: Firefox/Safari plans, Gemini in Chrome support (Google only
      said "will soon").
    </p>
    <p>Facts done. Now the part that's opinion — say so out loud.</p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide class="center" autoplay timing="550">
  <h2 class="main-gradient" style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
    What if nobody looks at your interface anymore?
  </h2>
  <p-fragment>
    <p style="font-size: 0.7em; opacity: 0.8;">
      No layout. No hover. No dark mode. Just <code>bookTable()</code>.
    </p>
  </p-fragment>

  <PNotes>
    <p>Slow down. Let the question sit — same energy as the opening hinge.</p>
    <p>
      Everything we built in this talk — layers, tokens, light-dark(), fluid space,
      container queries — an agent calling bookTable() sees none of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the pathos moment: not a threat, an honest "what if". This is my
      opinion, not a roadmap.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3 class="main-gradient">The end of interfaces?</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      🖼️ <span>Today the interface is </span><span class="main-sub-gradient"
        >the only way in</span
      >
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🤖 <span>Tomorrow, maybe </span><span class="main-sub-gradient"
        >one of the ways in</span
      >
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      ✍️ <span>We'll design </span><span class="main-sub-gradient"
        >intent and trust</span
      ><span>, not just screens</span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      For thirty years the screen was the only door into a product: if you
      wanted to book a table, you had to go through our layout, our buttons,
      our flow.
    </p>
    <p>
      If agents can call the action directly, the screen stops being the only
      door. Maybe that's the end of interfaces as we design them today.
    </p>
    <p>
      But someone still has to name the actions, describe them, decide what an
      agent may do alone and when the human must confirm, and show the human
      what happened. That's design. Just not only pixels.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide class="center" autoplay timing="550">
  <h2 class="main-gradient" style="margin-block-end: 0.3em">
    Not the end of design
  </h2>
  <p-fragment>
    <p style="font-size: 0.7em; opacity: 0.8;">
      Maybe the end of designing only for eyes.
    </p>
  </p-fragment>

  <PNotes>
    <p>The closing line of the epilogue. Pause, then thank you.</p>
    <p>
      Soft landing for the room: the craft doesn't disappear, the audience
      widens — humans and agents.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<style lang="scss">
  [support] {
    li {
      margin-block-end: 0.3em;
    }
  }
</style>
